Outcome-focused and person-centred practice in health and
social care

E309U2

72

Careers in health and social care are some of the most
important in the world. For various reasons throughout our
lives, and at different stages of life, some people need support
to develop and maintain their quality of life.  Health and social
care workers work independently and as part of wider teams to
provide professional health care and support to those in need.
Health and social care provides a broad career sector, with
options of employment within unique and dynamic
environments, working with individuals with a spectrum of
needs at different stages of life.

This unit introduces learners to the value of person-centred
practice, through application of how provision and practitioners’
roles meet the needs of individuals, and how practice is
underpinned by legislation and standards.  Learners will also
gain an understanding of safeguarding and types of abuse. They
will apply their knowledge of modern-day challenges to health
and well-being which individuals face, and the impact of these
on the health and social care sector.
Learners will need to draw on the following knowledge and
understanding from Unit 1 in order to complete the Unit 2
assessment:
•  growth and development across the lifespan
•  physical, intellectual, emotional and social aspects of

development

life factors

lifestyle choices

life events

the definitions of health, illness and disease and well-being

•

•

•

•

•  how to support individuals to take responsibility for their

own health and well-being.

