2.1.2
2.1.3

Content

Amplification

2.1.1
Key theories and how they
impact on care needs,
outcomes and
development across the
lifespan

Learners should know and understand the following key theories and
how they impact on care needs, outcomes and development across
the lifespan:

Maslow – hierarchy of needs: an individual's behaviour is based on
their motivation to satisfy their needs (basic and complex needs).
Failure to satisfy lower level needs results in an inability to meet higher
level needs.

2.1.2
Fundamental human needs
across the lifespan

Erikson – psychosocial development: an individual progresses through
eight stages of psychosocial development, with each stage
characterised by a psychosocial crisis to be overcome.

Learners should know and understand the following range of human
needs:

Physical: health, protection, care, food, shelter, acquisition of skills,
exercise, relaxation

Intellectual (cognitive): opportunities to learn, education, work,
creative expression, mental stimulation

Emotional: affection, love, respect, identity, self-esteem,
enjoyment/happiness, mental well-being, autonomy independence,
equality, spiritual

Social: friendship, belonging, participation/inclusion.

2.1.3
The changing needs of
physical care and
emotional support

Learners should know and understand that the following physical care
and emotional support needs change throughout the lifespan:

•

changes to needs due to expected/unexpected life events: starting
school, redundancy, pregnancy, divorce, bereavement

•  changes to needs for individuals living with:

•  chronic diseases; arthritis, asthma, Coronary Heart Disease

(CHD), diabetes

•  physical disabilities; mobility and sensory
•  mental illness; mood and anxiety disorders

