  "type": "4 marks — Describe with Example",
  "marks": "Up to 2 marks per strategy, applied to the GP surgery context. Education through information and advice (2 marks): 1 mark for naming the strategy; 1 mark for a specific GP surgery example (e.g. the practice provides patients with written information and a referral to an online health programme about managing pre-diabetes, giving them the knowledge and tools to make dietary and exercise changes). Encouraging use of personal strengths and informal networks (2 marks): 1 mark for naming the strategy; 1 mark for a specific example (e.g. the GP asks the patient who in their household or friendship group could walk with them regularly, helping them identify a support network that already exists rather than referring them to a paid service). Prevention and control also accepted with equivalent development."
 },
 {
  "text": "Sanjay is 52 years old and works in a call centre. He was recently told his blood pressure is borderline high and his BMI is 28.1. His GP wants to support him to make lifestyle changes rather than immediately prescribing medication.\n\nDiscuss how the GP could use health promotion strategies to support Sanjay to take responsibility for his own health.",
  "type": "6 marks — Discuss",
  "marks": "Mastering (5–6 marks): Addresses at least two health promotion strategies with developed reasoning, both linked to Sanjay's specific circumstances (borderline hypertension, overweight BMI, sedentary call-centre job). Education (Sanjay needs information about the link between his lifestyle and his readings, in a format he can act on — because without understanding the connection, he is unlikely to change); strengths-based (asking Sanjay what he enjoys and what small changes he feels able to make builds on his agency rather than imposing a plan); prevention and control (teaching Sanjay to monitor his own blood pressure at home gives him a tool to track his own progress). Considers challenges: Sanjay's sedentary work and possibly limited time for exercise. Uses 'because' and 'therefore'.\n\nSecure (3–4 marks): Addresses at least one strategy with development and some reference to Sanjay.\n\nDeveloping (2 marks): Names a strategy but limited development; limited link to Sanjay.\n\nEmerging (1 mark): Names a strategy with no development."
 },
 {
  "text": "Evaluate the extent to which raising awareness through national health campaigns is an effective strategy for promoting health and well-being across the population.",
  "type": "10 marks — Evaluate (Extended Response)",
  "marks": "Mastering (9–10 marks): Balanced evaluation of national campaigns as a health promotion strategy. Strengths: reach — a well-resourced national campaign (e.g. NHS Better Health) can reach millions of people through television, social media and GP signposting, a scale no individual practitioner can match; they raise awareness across the population, meaning even people who do not access health services may be reached; they normalise health-seeking behaviour and positive lifestyle choices. Limitations: awareness does not guarantee behaviour change — knowing that exercise is good does not mean people exercise more; campaigns may not reach the most deprived communities who face structural barriers to health (cost, access, time, housing); they may be more effective for already-motivated individuals and less so for those who need more personalised support. Comparison: campaigns are more effective when combined with local delivery, individual education and strengths-based approaches — the evidence shows that population-level messaging plus individual support is more effective than either alone. Conclusion: national campaigns are a valuable and necessary component of health promotion but are insufficient on their own; their effectiveness is maximised when they are integrated with local and individual-level support.\n\nSecure (6–8 marks): Addresses most strengths and limitations with some development and a conclusion.\n\nDeveloping (3–5 marks): Notes some strengths and limitations; limited conclusion.\n\nEmerging (1–2 marks): One-sided or very brief response."
 }
